{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:rstar:reconciler","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:30251fb356908ae598f61d3ff9eb6af10e6508db840776dc090bf57f870bd3ba","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":1.15},{"probability":0.5,"value":2.15},{"probability":0.9,"value":3.15}]},"questionHash":"sha256:20d8e05d3bea415eb4452ff8b599c74a3c3ce1a1b0c2ce11dda57a4e19490197","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq4","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"ANCHOR. All five panelists independently recomputed the G7 GDP-weighted level and landed at 1.76-2.07% against the brief's US TIPS 2.60% — Fermi's normalized build (US 0.522, JP 0.115, DE 0.104, UK 0.077, FR 0.075, IT 0.062, CA 0.045 on 2.60/0.50/0.90/1.50/1.20/1.70/1.70 = 1.896%) is the cleanest, and he correctly caught the Modeler's weights summing to 1.03 (so his 1.96% is 6bp overstated). The RED-TEAM MERELY CONTRARIAN-POSED on this and I do not move: calling three identical downward resolutions \"anchoring on each other's framing\" mislabels verifiable arithmetic as a shared prior, and his own market-FX recomputation moves the level only ~15-30bp, not 85bp. I DO give his weighting-basis point partial credit — a sovereign yield is a local nominal market price and inherits nothing from a PPP conversion, so the standard construction is market-FX nominal GDP (US to ~0.60-0.64) — and split 50/50 PPP vs market-FX for a starting anchor of ~1.98%.\nSTRUCTURE. My largest single structural adoption is the FERMI panelist's \"REPRESSION IS A BRANCH, NOT A SUBTRACTION\", and it is a confirmed hit against all three Wave-1 builds. Adding a +33 to +38bp debt-supply premium and then subtracting an independent expected repression wedge (-20 / -33 / -55bp) double-counts the same shock in opposite directions: repression is the policy response to, and the suppressor of, precisely the premium it is netted against, so the two are strongly negatively correlated, cannot be summed as independent components, and the additive treatment both pulls the central down and FALSELY NARROWS the distribution. The Japan 1990-2020 counterexample the outside-view panelists cite (debt 65->250% of GDP with falling real yields) does not refute the supply elasticity; it relocates it into the repression branch.\nr* (G7-weighted, 2032-36) = 1.05%, from ~0.70-0.75% today. I move UP from the Wave-1 0.75-0.95 band on one genuine red-team hit and stop well short of his 1.55% on a coherence failure. THE HIT: the Modeler explicitly cancels +35bp of life-cycle DECUMULATION FLOW against -20bp of longer-retirement-horizon DESIRED-WEALTH stock effect and reports r* flat; the desired-wealth channel was priced in during the 1990-2020 accumulation phase — it is why rates fell then — while the drawdown is the part still ahead, so netting a realized stock effect against an unrealized flow double-counts the past. Worth +15-20bp. THE COHERENCE FAILURE: his r* = 1.55% is +85bp in ten years, which with coupled g falling ~85-90bp makes (r−g) worsen ~1.75pp, overshooting 2A's locked ~+1.0pp, and delivers that +1.0pp THROUGH r* in direct contradiction of 2A's clause \"even where a frictionless r* EASES\". A locked marginal beats a lens. My deltas in bp: dissaving flow, un-netted, +25; a minority booking of public-debt absorption +12; China saving-glut drain into pension subsidies past 15% of fiscal expenditure +10; Japan/euro r* normalization +10; AI/grid/physical capex +5; precautionary saving under labour-income uncertainty -15; cognitive-compression saving-glut-of-the-rich -4 (certainty-equivalent on a 38% AI-Q5 marginal, not the Modeler's -9 booked as certainty); post-bust investment collapse inside the window -12. Net +31.\nTHE AI/GRID ARROW IS KILLED, and this is the effort brief's own temptation. Fermi's arithmetic on the LOCKED AI-Q3 marginal: 998-365 = 633 incremental TWh = ~72 GW average delivered power = ~96 GW IT capacity ≈ $1.06T over 11 years = ~$96B/yr of data-centre capex plus $35-45B/yr of generation and grid ≈ $135B/yr = 0.15% of bloc GDP and ~0.6% of GFCF, worth +5bp on r* at a -35bp-per-pp loanable-funds slope (+15bp trebled for robots and non-DC physical AI). The Modeler's +40bp needs 1.0-1.5pp of GDP, 7-10x what the locked marginal supports. The capex channel must enter through interconnection queues, transformer and turbine lead times and electricity PRICES, not the saving-investment balance.\nREAL TERM PREMIUM, no-repression branch = 1.60%. By identity, today's premium = 1.98 − 0.72 = 1.26. Deltas: debt supply +25 (not +33, because I moved +12 of absorption into r* and will not double-count; G7-weighted debt/GDP +15pp at ~2.2bp/pp after Laubach's 3-4bp is haircut for regulatory-captive domestic demand); convenience-yield erosion, a balance-sheet- and repo-haircut-contingent levered basis-trade marginal bid, and reduced central-bank duration absorption +20; the AUDITOR's genuinely underrated asset-side aging channel — closed DB schemes reaching full funding and buying out, withdrawing a price-insensitive long-duration bid +10; decelerating China as a lower-growth, lower-inflation-volatility anchor on global duration demand -15. Sum 1.66, haircut to 1.60 (premium estimates rarely sustain above ~1.6pp and 2032-36 sits after the capex boom). Branch N level = 1.05 + 1.60 = 2.65%, sigma 0.55.\nREPRESSION BRANCH: p = 0.38, level 1.50%, sigma 0.45. Probability between the red-team's 0.35 and the Wave-1 0.45-0.60, accepting his argument that binding US 10y YCC spends the inflation credibility that is the asset being defended, and that the historical base rate rests on pre-independence 1945-80, Japanese YCC, and euro-area SPREAD management — a different instrument on a different part of the curve. I also accept his SECOND, MOST UNDERRATED HIT: repression operates on NOMINAL yields while CQ4 subtracts a breakeven, and Japan under YCC 2016-2024 held 10y nominals at 0-0.25% while frequently printing a POSITIVE measured real yield because the yen BEI sat near zero. So the wedge on the MEASURED real yield applies only when paired with a tolerated inflation overshoot — roughly half the branch. That is why I put branch R at 1.50% (premium ~1.00 never clearing into the market, explicit wedge -0.55) rather than Fermi's 1.10%.\nMIXTURE. Mean = 0.38 x 1.50 + 0.62 x 2.65 = 2.21. Median by CDF: at 2.20, F = 0.38xPhi(1.556) + 0.62xPhi(-0.818) = 0.357 + 0.128 = 0.485; at 2.25, F = 0.507 → median ~2.23. Measurement net: I reject the Auditor's claimed +10bp — Fermi showed it cannot stand once his own CPI-swap-versus-TIPS-breakeven item (swaps trade 20-35bp above the breakeven, so using swaps LOWERS the measured US real yield by 10-18bp on the aggregate) is applied against Japan's illiquid BEI bias and Italy's BTP-Bund credit spread entering as though it were a real rate — honest net ~-5bp. P50 = 2.15 after a further small haircut for the PPP reading of the weighting basis having the stronger textual claim. P10 = 1.15 (F(1.20) ≈ 0.113 on the mixture, less the artefact): branch R binding in the US and euro area simultaneously after an AI-led equity shock with post-bust precautionary saving swamping the decumulation flow. P90 = 3.15 (F(3.20) ≈ 0.901): no repression, convenience yield erodes hard, Japan and Europe converge fully. Five-year ARITHMETIC averaging is what keeps P90 below 3.5 — the Auditor is right that this is a level-dominated question that kills the spike tails.\nPATH CHECK the red-team won: the Modeler wrote that \"most of the 2032-36 change is Japanese and euro-area convergence UP\" — worth ~+45-50bp weighted (JP 0.5->1.8, DE 0.8->1.6, FR, IT, UK, CA) — and then neutralized it with an assumed US mean reversion to 1.9-2.1 whose only mechanism is \"the 2026 term premium normalizes\", while 2A says the premium's drivers INTENSIFY through 2031 (US debt 142%, net interest to 3% of GDP, convenience-yield erosion, basis-trade fragility, P95 Debt-at-Risk 117% rising 2.4pp on a 20% AI-led equity shock). You cannot hold both. I keep ~+25-35bp of net convergence after partial US reversion, and it is the second reason my P50 exceeds the Wave-1 centre of 1.88%.\nVARIANCE ATTRIBUTION: between-branch = 0.38x0.62x(2.65-1.50)^2 = 0.312; within = 0.38x0.2025 + 0.62x0.3025 = 0.265; total 0.576 (sd 0.76). REPRESSION BRANCH SWITCH 54%, real term premium 27%, r* 19%. I side with Fermi's ordering against BOTH the Wave-1 unanimous premium-dominant verdict and the red-team's r*-dominant claim: once repression is modelled as the bimodal policy switch it actually is, the switch carries the variance, and the r* level carries least precisely because its demographic sub-channels partially cancel even after de-netting.\n2A DISTINCTION, CHECKED ARITHMETICALLY: r goes 1.98 -> 2.15 (+17bp) while coupled g goes ~2.7%/yr realized 2015-24 -> 1.85% (-85bp), so (r−g) worsens ~+1.02pp, matching 2A's ~+1.0pp, with r* contributing +33bp — a minority. The claim is satisfied by a modestly rising r* plus fiscal arithmetic and premium, which is the distinction the brief demands and which the red-team's build violates.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:53.921Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"G7 GDP-weighted 10y sovereign real yield (10y nominal benchmark minus 10y inflation swap / breakeven, computed on the same seven benchmarks) averages between 1.70% and 2.60% in EVERY one of 2030, 2031 and 2032, with no G7 sovereign having adopted explicit yield-curve control, a statutory captive-holding mandate for banks or insurers, or a central-bank 'market functioning' purchase programme above 3% of GDP.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"This is the signpost most specific to ME, because my distinguishing claim is not the level but the BIMODALITY: a three-year run inside a 90bp band with the policy branch unfired says the distribution is unimodal and my 54% branch-switch variance attribution is wrong. CQ4 collapses to roughly P10 1.60 / P50 2.20 / P90 2.90 (narrower, not shifted), the variance attribution reverts to premium-dominant (~50%) as the Wave-1 panel had it, CQ5 falls from 28 to ~22 because the bimodal-switch channel that fattens the rate-vol distribution disappears, and ~4pp moves from CQ6-C into B.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"The New York Fed HLW r* estimate for the United States (or the Richmond Fed Lubik-Matthes companion) is published at or below 1.0% for both the 2028 and 2029 vintages with no upward revision to the 2024-27 history, WHILE the US 10y TIPS real yield (FRED DFII10) averages below 1.70% over any rolling twelve months in the absence of any announced YCC, duration-shortening or captive-buyer mandate.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Kills my partial concession to the red-team that the filter is revising into a regime break, and vindicates the Wave-1 low-and-flat r* I moved away from. r* falls from 1.05% to ~0.80%, CQ4 P50 drops ~30bp to ~1.85 with P90 to ~2.80, CQ6-D rises from 13 to ~18 and CQ6-A falls ~2pp (cheap duration signals abundant saving, which is the demand-shortfall regime), and my CQ2 conjunct A rises ~4pp as the duration-price-versus-price-level substitution reverses.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Japan's 10y JGB nominal yield stays below 2.25% AND Germany's 10y Bund below 3.00% on a twelve-month average, with the Bank of Japan holding a policy rate at or below 1.0%, in both 2031 and 2032.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Removes the Japan/euro convergence-upward component worth ~+25-35bp of my CQ4 recomposition — the component the Mechanism Modeler described in words and then neutralized, and that I restored. CQ4 P50 falls ~30bp to ~1.85 and the whole distribution shifts down without changing shape; CQ5 falls ~3pp through the MOVE level channel; CQ3 and CQ6 are essentially unchanged, because this is a price signpost, not a quantity one. It would also mean my 50/50 PPP-versus-market-FX weighting split mattered less than the component levels.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Operational data-centre electricity across OECD-38 + China (strict operational definition) reaches 1,000 TWh/yr or more in 2036 WHILE cumulative announced-buildout impairments and cancellations exceed 20% of announced hyperscaler capex — i.e. the quantity arrives AND the write-downs arrive together.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2036\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"CONFIRMS the red-team's reversal that I adopted against the Wave-1 unanimous direction — that AI-Q3 is a quantity question gated by interconnection queues, transformers and licensed electricians rather than a financing question gated by the term premium, so a higher cost of capital changes who owns and who writes down the asset, not the megawatts. The converse observation (Q3 below 900 TWh with impairments above 20%) would falsify it and restore the Wave-1 coupling direction, moving my coupling note's Q3 call from flat-to-up back to down and raising the weight I place on the loanable-funds channel in r* from +5bp toward +15bp.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"An MSCI World peak-to-trough drawdown of >=25% occurs and G7 GDP-weighted 10y nominal sovereign yields fall by >=100bp from the equity peak date to the trough date — the bond hedge works emphatically, not marginally — while the trailing MOVE average since Jan 2027 is still below 100.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2033\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Consumes CQ5's most likely qualifying episode with a non-qualifying case and confirms the Wave-1 disinflationary-bust premise against the red-team's credit-event argument that I partly accepted. CQ5 falls from 28 to ~13 (short remaining window, both conjuncts weakened simultaneously). It also implies abundant precautionary saving, so CQ4 P50 falls ~30bp and my repression branch probability falls from 0.38 toward 0.28 (a sovereign that still rallies in stress needs no policy backstop); CQ6-D rises from 13 to ~18 at B's and C's expense.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run1 wave 3 reconciler seat, effort 'rstar'."}}